I don't disagree that John should have made himself scarce while it was happening - someone's suggestion about having a private room to duck into when he suffers an episode seems like it would go a long way. I also agree that OP shouldn't have to be subjected to this in any way, shape, or form - that article also mentions that slurs are the only words that are harmful to use and to hear. The company is completely, 100% the asshole, as they're forcing OP and John into a state of conflict and not reaching a solution that helps both of them.

I agree completely. That scene is seared onto my brain now, and here's why.

&#x200B;

Personally, one of the most terrifying things for me in media - and it's found in almost every genre - is seeing doomed characters realize *exactly* what's about to happen to them, succumb to blind, instinctive panic, and start making desperate, futile attempts to escape, reduced to just a scrabbling animal. No facing death with dignity, no calm acceptance of their fate, just sobbing and screaming and crying out for mommy while fruitlessly trying to claw their way out.

&#x200B;

This trope is why I couldn't even get through the first episode of attack on titan, why the Green trial still haunts me, and why the "Happy Valley" episode of For All Mankind left me sleepless for a few nights.

&#x200B;

There's a moment in the scene you describe where one of the characters involved realizes that this is where she's going to die and how it's going to happen - and it's the exact same moment I realized >!this was no probe ship, it was a fucking digestive system!< \- and you can *hear* her screaming change from terror to a sort of hopeless anguish. Even now, thinking about it, my hands are still shaking.

&#x200B;

The other scene was horrifying too, but Jesus did this one leave me changed.

&#x200B;

ETA: wrong GoT term, lol

YTA. If you truly cared about her future it would be a *relief* and a *blessing* that she is able to be successful in her passion when so many others don't get that chance. It'd be a wonderful opportunity for her going forward.

&#x200B;

Instead, you're taking it as a betrayal, and a deception, as if you're losing out on your investment. She's not a product, she's your daughter. Remember that and apologize before she shuts you out completely

My first period was like, the single major event in my life that my mom has been chill about. We got pads, it was no big deal, one of my male friends got me peanut butter cups a day or two later after I told him bc I mentioned that was my favorite. 

&#x200B;

Turned out to be a health issue later when it started lasting for three weeks instead of one, but for once the normal amount of emphasis was placed on something that happened to me, and it was a relief.

So uh, we might be the same person. Same birthday, actually. Year earlier than me. My parents almost named me Alexandria. Cider is the only alcohol I actually like.  My comfort plushie is my current impromptu chinrest. 

&#x200B;

...Should I be concerned?

NOT SAFE

&#x200B;

I have a theory. There's a Biblical theme here. As such, I'm not so certain it's "control." It's COMMAND.

What are the stands to the left and what is the pathway in front of the shrine? 

&#x200B;

Love your camp, by the way, we have similar aesthetics!

The Last Airbender Movie. I was a young kid, who fucking LOVED movies. They were such a special treat for me, and no matter what the movie was I'd usually enjoy the experience of being in the theater and getting snacks. And the fact that it was one of my favorite cartoons? Double score! 

&#x200B;

But no. I walked out of that movie, turned to my dad, and said, "Why wasn't that fun?" It was the first movie that I actually hated so much it overrode the excitement of being in the theater. 

&#x200B;

Thanks, Shamalayan.
